Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Period live-action, autumn 1897. BUCK, a four-year-old, one-hundred-forty-pound St. Bernard–Scotch shepherd mix, surveys the Santa Clara estate. He has a broad chest, dark sable coat, brown muzzle and brows, and a white chest blaze.

(SOUND: birds in the orchards, children laughing, water splashing in the cement tank)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every strong-muscled, warm, long-haired dog. Men had found a yellow metal in the Arctic darkness.

Buck swims with JUDGE MILLER'S sons, carries a grandchild across the lawn, and settles beside the Judge on the veranda. The smaller dogs yield as he passes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Over this great demesne Buck ruled. He was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

At the orchard gate, MANUEL, a lean gardener's helper with anxious eyes, hides a Chinese lottery ticket inside his coat and watches Buck.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

A solitary lamp illuminates the flag station. MANUEL leads the trusting Buck to a waiting buyer and accepts a handful of coins.

(SOUND: coins chinking, telegraph wire humming, an approaching locomotive)

Manuel doubles a stout rope beneath Buck's brass collar and gives the ends to the buyer.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The rope tightens. Buck launches at the buyer, but the men throw him onto his back and throttle him until his eyes glaze.

(SOUND: Buck's strangled growl, rope fibers straining, the locomotive whistle shrieking)

The train stops only long enough for Buck's limp body to be hurled aboard. Manuel remains beneath the lamp as the kidnapped king disappears into the night.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

A high-walled yard contains Buck's splintered shipping crate. SMITH, a stout dog dealer in a sagging red sweater, approaches with a hatchet and club.

(SOUND: hatchet blows splitting the crate, Buck snarling behind the slats)

Buck bursts through the opening. Smith's club catches him in midair and smashes him onto the dirt.

Buck rises and charges again. Again the club drops him.

(SOUND: the club striking, Buck's roar, watching dogs barking from their crates)

After repeated charges, Buck crumples, blood flecking his dark coat and white chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten. He knew that. But he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

Smith sets down the club, brings water, and cautiously strokes Buck's battered head.

SMITH
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck endures the touch, then drinks ravenously.

Days later, PERRAULT, a small, swarthy French-Canadian courier with a weazened face, studies Buck as government coins pass to Smith.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog!

Perrault leads Buck away with CURLY, a friendly, coal-black Newfoundland.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

Snow falls over the crowded northern beach. Buck steps from the ship, sinks into the white powder, snorts, and tastes it as it vanishes on his tongue.

(SOUND: surf under shore ice, wind snapping canvas, dockworkers laughing at Buck's first snow)

FRANCOIS, a large, dark-faced French-Canadian half-breed, brings Buck and Curly among the sled dogs. SPITZ, a powerful snow-white husky with pale eyes, watches from beside the traces.

Curly greets a wolfish husky. Without warning, it slashes her face and leaps away.

Curly rushes. The husky strikes her chest and knocks her down. A silent circle of dogs closes upon her.

(SOUND: Curly screaming beneath snarling bodies, Francois cursing, an axe haft thudding into the pack)

Francois scatters the dogs, but Curly lies lifeless in the bloodied snow. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue as though laughing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Buck would see to it that he never went down.

Francois straps Buck into a harness behind Spitz. Buck resists until the whip snaps and the traces jerk taut.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Buck watches the other dogs, lowers his broad chest, and pulls.

(SOUND: runners scraping, harness rings jingling, Francois's whip cracking above the team)

EXT. KLONDIKE TRAIL - DAY

Days blur beneath a white sky. Buck, leaner and iron-muscled beneath his dark sable coat, strains through drifts, eats frozen salmon in gulps, and learns to sleep beneath the snow.

(SOUND: sled bells, runners hissing over ice, dogs panting in the bitter wind)

When hunger gnaws, Buck steals bacon while Perrault's back is turned. Another dog receives the punishment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This first theft marked Buck as fit to survive in the hostile Northland. He stole because of the clamor of his stomach and stole secretly out of respect for club and fang.

Spitz shoulders Buck away from the lead and bares his teeth. Buck does not attack. He watches, waits, and hardens.

That night, Buck raises his muzzle toward a star and releases a long, wolflike howl.

(SOUND: Buck's howl gathering distant answers across the frozen country)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ancient song surged through him, and he came into his own again.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit flashes through moonlit drifts. Buck leads a roaring pack along the frozen creek while Spitz cuts silently across the bend.

(SOUND: sixty dogs in full cry, paws pounding snow, the rabbit's terrified breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is an ecstasy that marks the summit of life. It came to Buck, leading the pack, sounding the old wolf-cry, flying exultantly under the stars.

Spitz drops from the bank and breaks the rabbit's back.

(SOUND: the rabbit shrieking, the pack answering with a savage chorus)

Buck drives shoulder-first into Spitz. They roll apart and circle while the other dogs form a silent ring.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and springs clear. Buck attacks again and again, but Spitz's white throat remains guarded.

(SOUND: fangs clashing, breath rasping, blood pattering onto powdery snow)

Buck feints high, sweeps low, and crushes Spitz's left foreleg. He repeats the trick on the right.

Spitz staggers on two broken legs. The waiting circle tightens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes.

Buck's final shoulder strike topples Spitz. The pack surges over the fallen leader and swallows him from view.

The snarling ceases. Buck stands alone in the moonlight, bleeding but upright.

(SOUND: the wind returning across the silent creek)

EXT. YUKON TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Buck, his sable coat cut and bloodied, stands beside the fire while Francois examines his wounds. Spitz's place is empty.

(SOUND: firewood popping, harness buckles clinking, restless dogs whining)

FRANCOIS
Dat Spitz fight lak hell. An' dat Buck fight lak two hells.

Francois places an old husky in the lead. Buck drives it aside and claims the position.

Francois advances with a club. Buck retreats beyond its reach, neither fleeing nor surrendering.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois obeys. Buck immediately trots forward and submits to the lead harness.

(SOUND: traces snapping taut, sled bells bursting into rhythm)

Buck launches the team down the trail. Every dog falls into step behind him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership. The team recovered its solidarity and leaped as one dog in the traces.

EXT. SKAGUAY CAMP - DAY

Months of mail work have reduced Buck to a gaunt one hundred fifteen pounds. His once-glossy coat hangs loose, though his brown brows and white chest blaze remain unmistakable.

(SOUND: mail-sled bells, axes chopping, exhausted dogs coughing and panting)

HAL, a beardless youth wearing a cartridge belt, revolver, and hunting knife, buys the team with CHARLES, a pale middle-aged man with watery eyes and an upturned mustache. MERCEDES, pretty and soft in a blue wool coat and fur hat, directs them as they pile baggage onto the sled.

The load towers above the dogs.

HAL
Mush on there!

The team strains. The frozen sled does not move. Hal lashes them.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't! The poor dears! Why don't you pull hard? Then you wouldn't be whipped.

Hal breaks the runners loose. The overloaded sled lurches downhill, overturns, and scatters their belongings along the street.

(SOUND: tin dishes clattering, wood splintering, Hal shouting as he is dragged through the snow)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They cut the load, added more dogs, and worked the trip out with a pencil. It was all very simple. But they did not know how to work dogs, and they did not know how to work themselves.

EXT. KLONDIKE TRAIL - DAY

Spring sunlight glitters over rotten ice and running water. Buck and the surviving dogs stagger as skeletons in harness while Mercedes rides atop the load.

(SOUND: meltwater trickling, returning birds singing, Hal's whip cutting through the spring air)

Food dwindles to strips of frozen horsehide. Dogs collapse and do not rise. Hal cuts them from the traces and drives the rest onward with his club.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Amid all this bursting, rending, throbbing of awakening life, like wayfarers to death staggered the two men, the woman, and the huskies.

Buck falls, rises beneath blows, and pulls until the trail brings them to a riverside camp.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

The exhausted team lies before JOHN THORNTON, a lean, weathered frontiersman with a brown beard, buckskin coat, and slight limp. He whittles a birch axe handle beside the thawing river.

(SOUND: swollen water grinding beneath ice, birch curls whispering from Thornton's knife)

THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal orders the dogs up. Buck remains motionless.

The whip lashes Buck's ribs. He does not rise. Hal exchanges the whip for his club and beats him harder.

(SOUND: the club thudding against Buck's body, Mercedes sobbing, river ice groaning nearby)

Thornton explodes from beside the fire and hurls Hal backward.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Hal draws his hunting knife. Thornton cracks the axe handle across his knuckles, takes the knife, and cuts Buck's traces.

(SOUND: knife slicing leather, the severed harness dropping into the snow)

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes drive the surviving team onto the river. Thornton kneels beside Buck and searches his starved body for broken bones.

Far out, the sled's rear drops. Hal's gee-pole jerks skyward.

(SOUND: Mercedes screaming, ice splitting with a rifle-shot crack)

A whole section collapses. Humans, dogs, and sled vanish into black water.

(SOUND: the river thundering beneath the widening hole, then only drifting ice)

Thornton and Buck look at one another.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Weeks later, Buck's muscles have swelled and his dark sable coat shines again. SKEET, a small red Irish setter, cleans his last wounds while NIG, a huge black bloodhound–deerhound mix with gentle eyes, rests nearby.

(SOUND: open river flowing, bees humming, birds calling through new leaves)

Thornton takes Buck's broad head between his hands, presses his brow against Buck's, and shakes him roughly. Buck seizes Thornton's hand between his jaws without breaking the skin.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. This man had saved his life and saw to the welfare of his dogs as if they were his own children.

A distant wolf howl drifts from the forest. Buck turns toward it, then lies at Thornton's feet.

EXT. DAWSON MAIN STREET - DAY

Months later, Buck weighs one hundred fifty pounds, his broad chest, powerful shoulders, dark sable coat, brown brows, and white blaze restored. Thornton, still brown-bearded and buckskinned, faces MATTHEWSON beside a frozen freight sled.

(SOUND: miners murmuring, harness chains rattling, a stove roaring inside the nearby saloon)

Twenty flour sacks, each stamped FIFTY POUNDS, are stacked on the sled.

THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

Buck is harnessed alone. Thornton kneels, takes Buck's head in both hands, and rests cheek against cheek.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck closes his jaws gently around Thornton's mitten, then releases him.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck plunges right. The load quivers.

(SOUND: frozen runners crackling beneath the snow)

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck sweeps left. The sled pivots, breaking free of the ice.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole body forward. His paws claw parallel grooves into the packed snow. The sled jerks an inch, then another.

(SOUND: traces creaking, runners grinding, the crowd holding a breathless silence)

Buck finds his rhythm. The half-ton load moves steadily until he passes the firewood marking one hundred yards.

Thornton gives the stopping command. The street erupts.

(SOUND: hundreds of miners cheering, hats and mittens striking the snow)

Thornton falls to his knees and embraces Buck, tears streaming through his brown beard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The winnings paid Thornton's debts and made possible a journey into the virgin East, where miners had not yet appeared.

EXT. PLACER VALLEY CAMP - DAY

Months later, a spruce-bough lodge stands in an unmapped valley. Thornton works beside PETE, a stocky red-bearded woodsman, and HANS, a tall blond frontiersman in a gray fur cap.

Buck, huge and wolf-like but still marked by his brown brows and white chest, watches them fill moose-hide sacks with gold.

(SOUND: sluice water rushing, shovels biting gravel, gold rattling in a pan)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They found, not the Lost Cabin, but a shallow placer where the gold showed like yellow butter across the bottom of the washing-pan.

With nothing to haul, Buck ranges farther into the forest. A long, distinct howl silences the camp.

(SOUND: a lone timber wolf calling from deep among the trees)

Buck looks once at Thornton, then vanishes between the spruce trunks.

EXT. FOREST CREEK - NIGHT

Moonlight fills an open place beside the creek. A long, lean gray timber wolf sits with its nose pointed skyward.

(SOUND: the timber wolf's howl echoing through the gorge, creek water slipping beneath roots)

Buck stalks into the clearing. The WILD BROTHER flees, but Buck overtakes and corners him against a timber jam.

Instead of attacking, Buck lowers his head and circles with cautious, friendly advances. At last, they touch noses.

The two run side by side through the twilight, over the divide and into a land of streams.

At dawn, Buck stops to drink. The thought of Thornton pulls his gaze back toward camp.

The Wild Brother whines and urges him onward. Buck turns home.

(SOUND: the Wild Brother's mournful howl fading behind Buck)

EXT. MOOSE VALLEY - DAY

A great bull moose towers above Buck, an arrow protruding from its flank. Buck darts beneath the fourteen-point antlers and cuts the wounded bull away from its herd.

(SOUND: the bull bellowing, hooves smashing brush, Buck's sharp challenge)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is a patience of the wild, dogged, tireless, persistent as life itself. It belonged to Buck as he clung to the flank of the herd.

For four days Buck denies the bull food, water, and rest. At last the exhausted moose falls beneath him.

Buck feeds and sleeps beside the kill, then rises abruptly. He scents the wind.

(SOUND: birds falling silent, a distant human chant carried on the breeze)

Buck races toward Thornton.

EXT. PLACER VALLEY CAMP - DAY

Buck, enormous and blood-streaked from the hunt, slips through an unnaturally silent forest. He finds Nig dead beneath an arrow, then Hans lying facedown near the clearing.

(SOUND: a sing-song Yeehat chant, fire crackling among the wrecked lodge poles)

YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the ruined camp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For the last time in his life Buck allowed passion to usurp cunning and reason. It was because of his great love for John Thornton.

Buck erupts from the trees with a roar. He tears through the foremost hunters before they can raise their weapons.

(SOUND: Buck's lionlike roar, bowstrings snapping, hunters crying out in panic)

Arrows fly into the tangled hunters. Buck strikes and vanishes, strikes and vanishes, until the survivors scatter into the forest.

(SOUND: fleeing footsteps crashing through brush, the chant replaced by terrified shouts)

Buck returns to the desolation. Pete lies dead in his blankets. Skeet rests at the edge of the mining pool, faithful to the last.

Thornton's struggle is written in the torn earth. Buck follows his scent into the muddy pool, but no scent leads out.

(SOUND: water dripping from a broken sluice, Buck's low whine in the sudden stillness)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, as a passing out and away from the lives of the living, he knew. John Thornton was dead. It left a great void in him which food could not fill.

Buck settles beside the pool and waits.

EXT. PLACER VALLEY CAMP - NIGHT

A full moon shines over the ruined lodge. Buck remains beside Thornton's pool, his dark coat silvered by the light.

(SOUND: sharp wolf yelps approaching through the valley, wind stirring the spruce boughs)

A wolf pack pours into the clearing. The boldest wolf springs at Buck.

Buck breaks its neck with one flash of his jaws. Three more attack, and he slashes them back.

(SOUND: fangs snapping, wolves snarling, paws scraping gravel)

The pack withdraws. The Wild Brother steps from among them and approaches cautiously.

Buck's rigid body softens. They touch noses.

An old, battle-scarred wolf sits, raises his muzzle, and begins the long howl. The pack joins him.

(SOUND: the many-noted call swelling beneath the moon)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

Buck raises his own muzzle and howls. The wolves spring toward the forest, and Buck runs beside the Wild Brother at the head of the pack.

EXT. NORTHLAND VALLEY - WINTER NIGHT

Years later, a wolf pack crosses the snow beneath a glimmering aurora. Younger wolves bear Buck's brown brows and white chest markings.

(SOUND: paws whispering over powder, the pack's voices rising through the frozen valley)

Buck leads them, gigantic above his fellows, his sable coat silver beneath the moon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack. Each fall there is a valley which they never enter.

Buck crests a ridge, throws back his great head, and releases the ancient song.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
